{
  "source": "Prompts/critical-thinking-v11.mp4",
  "duration": 197.8453125,
  "segments": [
    {
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 4.36,
      "text": "This graphic lays out a basic equation for how we process information."
    },
    {
      "start": 5.24,
      "end": 11.22,
      "text": "Having knowledge is essential, but what you choose to do with that knowledge is what actually dictates your results."
    },
    {
      "start": 12.16,
      "end": 14.84,
      "text": "Look at the left side. Knowledge forms your baseline."
    },
    {
      "start": 15.66,
      "end": 21.14,
      "text": "It helps you understand a subject and recognize when something in a text or a prompt doesn't add up."
    },
    {
      "start": 21.78,
      "end": 23.5,
      "text": "On the right side is the active component."
    },
    {
      "start": 23.5,
      "end": 31.98,
      "text": "Critical thinking means you question a claim, examine the evidence, and consider alternative explanations before you decide what to do next."
    },
    {
      "start": 32.86,
      "end": 37.58,
      "text": "When you combine these two halves, the result is better questions and better decisions."
    },
    {
      "start": 38.38,
      "end": 42.28,
      "text": "Be smarter than the tool. It isn't don't trust anything either."
    },
    {
      "start": 42.72,
      "end": 46.72,
      "text": "It's the habit of asking what would have to be true for a claim to hold up."
    },
    {
      "start": 46.72,
      "end": 56.3,
      "text": "In 2015, news outlets around the world publish stories about a scientific study claiming a delicious new method for losing weight."
    },
    {
      "start": 57.16,
      "end": 63.56,
      "text": "One front page ran the headline, slim by chocolate. The face value reaction was immediate. Sounds great."
    },
    {
      "start": 64.38,
      "end": 68.3,
      "text": "I believe it. But a critical thinking reaction takes a different path."
    },
    {
      "start": 68.94,
      "end": 76.3,
      "text": "Wait. What's behind the claim? You must force yourself to pause, especially when a claim matches what you want to believe."
    },
    {
      "start": 76.3,
      "end": 83.0,
      "text": "The study itself actually existed. However, it was deliberately designed to be scientifically flimsy."
    },
    {
      "start": 83.84,
      "end": 86.68,
      "text": "This diagram illustrates the primary flaw in the data."
    },
    {
      "start": 87.36,
      "end": 95.32,
      "text": "The researchers used a tiny sample size of only 15 participants, but they tracked 18 entirely different measurements for each person."
    },
    {
      "start": 95.92,
      "end": 100.96,
      "text": "With that many measurements in such a small group, a chance result could look like an important discovery."
    },
    {
      "start": 101.7,
      "end": 106.74,
      "text": "The supposed research institute backing the study was nothing more than a fabricated website."
    },
    {
      "start": 107.64,
      "end": 117.72,
      "text": "The true author was a journalist. He orchestrated the entire stunt to prove that bad science wrapped in a highly appealing headline will spread globally before anyone verifies the facts."
    },
    {
      "start": 118.54,
      "end": 126.38,
      "text": "Critical thinking requires the ability to read between those lines, particularly when our desires make us vulnerable to confirmation bias."
    },
    {
      "start": 126.38,
      "end": 134.34,
      "text": "This board outlines five habits you can build to sharpen your thinking. Every single one functions as a direct question."
    },
    {
      "start": 135.06,
      "end": 142.52,
      "text": "Habit one. Is it actually right? You check what evidence supports the claim and verify if that evidence genuinely aligns with the conclusion."
    },
    {
      "start": 143.44,
      "end": 150.98,
      "text": "Habit two. Do I know enough to judge? You have to recognize the limits of your own knowledge and identify exactly what you still need to learn."
    },
    {
      "start": 150.98,
      "end": 157.24,
      "text": "Habit three. What's missing? You actively hunt for omitted information or alternative ex..."
    },
    {
      "start": 157.24,
      "end": 165.62,
      "text": "Fourth. Why am I convinced? Question your own motives to see if you are swayed by hard evidence, confident wording, or wishful thinking."
    },
    {
      "start": 166.44,
      "end": 173.82,
      "text": "And fifth. What's my call? Finally, you decide what to believe or do, keeping the flexibility to change your mind when you learn more."
    },
    {
      "start": 173.82,
      "end": 181.06,
      "text": "These five questions work on any information you consume. They are especially critical when you use artificial intelligence."
    },
    {
      "start": 182.22,
      "end": 188.46,
      "text": "AI can help you find a flaw in your thinking or confidently repeat it. You need to judge which is happening."
    },
    {
      "start": 189.28,
      "end": 193.38,
      "text": "AI amplifies whatever you bring to it. Good thinking in. Sharper output."
    }
  ],
